 abolitionist movement, historians have often overlooked the great grassroots army of women who also fought to eliminate slavery. Here, Julie Roy Jeffrey explores the involvement of ordinary women — black and white — in the most significant reform movement prior to the Civil War. She offers a complex and compelling portrait of antebellum women’s activism, tracing its changing contours over time. For more than three decades, women raised money, carried petitions, created propaganda, sponsored lecture series, circulated newspapers, supported third-party movements, became public lecturers, and assisted fugitive slaves. Indeed, Jeffrey says, theirs was the day-to-day work that helped to keep abolitionism alive.","thumbnailUrl":"/book-covers/book-the-great-silent-army-of-abolitionism-ordinary-women-in-the-antislavery-movement-julie-roy-jeffr.jpg","readerProfile":{"readerLevel":"crossover","narrativeScore":0.066,"accessibilityScore":0,"scholarlyScore":0.196,"traits":[{"id":"scholarly","score":0.2,"confidence":"low","evidence":["catalog text contains \"historians\""]},{"id":"character_driven","score":0.18,"confidence":"low","evidence":["catalog text contains \"portrait of\""]}],"method":"deterministic_catalog_heuristics_v1"},"renownProfile":{"knowsBook":true,"publicFame":24,"criticalRenown":65,"controversy":8,"llmAffinity":78,"affinityResidual":4.87,"confidence":{"publicFame":0.88,"criticalRenown":0.82,"controversy":0.76,"llmAffinity":0.84},"tags":{"craft":"craft-of-explanation","evidence":"archival-research","stance":"revisionism"},"model":"gpt-5.6-luna","promptVersion":1},"experimentalSemanticProfile":{"centralFigures":[],"centralPlaces":[],"argument":{"present":true,"statement":"Women’s day-to-day activism helped sustain the abolitionist movement and advance efforts to eliminate slavery.","confidence":0.96},"academicOrientation":{"score":72,"confidence":0.9},"profileConfidence":0.95,"model":"gpt-5.6-luna","promptVersion":8,"inputHash":"b189dcbd2b3dae7daa660ec9fd05dcb1ad